            <ArticleTitle>Rapid recovery protocols for primary total hip arthroplasty can safely reduce length of stay without increasing readmissions.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>521-6</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.arth.2015.01.023</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S0883-5403(15)00037-6</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>We sought to investigate the impact of incremental perioperative recovery protocol changes on hospital LOS and readmission rates associated with primary THAs. A total of 1751 cases were assigned to one of four protocol cohorts across 13 years: traditional, enhanced pain management, early mobility, and rapid recovery (RR). LOS significantly decreased between sequential eras and by 52% between the traditional and RR pathways (IRR=0.48; 95% CI 0.44, 0.53; P&lt;0.0001) without an overall increase in 30-day readmission rates (P=0.13). The odds of readmission for THAs performed under the RR pathway were almost one-third those of the traditional era (OR=0.36; 95% CI 0.14, 0.93; P=0.04). Accelerated clinical care protocols should be considered for most patients undergoing primary THA.</AbstractText>
